#--: Overview

The #~"json" unit is a JSON (RFC 8259) value tree, parser, and
serializer. The tree is a reftype tagged sum (#~"Value")
mirroring the AST's union-of-carriers shape: scalars ride in the
arm payload, arrays and objects in carrier classes (#~"Arr",
#~"Obj"). Objects remember key insertion order, so serialization
is deterministic; lookup stays O(1). Integers and floats are
separate arms, so #~"i64" values (protocol ids, offsets)
round-trip exactly.

Accessed via qualified names only -- #~"json.parse ...",
#~"json.Value.intval ..."; there is no unqualified re-export.

#b:"The #~\"(Box T)\" arm rule, showcased." A union's arms hold
reftypes; a valtype arm is spelled #~"(Box T)" and #b:"collapses"
to its element. #~"Value" is the standard library's showcase of
the rule: the #~"boolean" / #~"intval" / #~"floatval" arms are
declared #~"(Box bool)" / #~"(Box i64)" / #~"(Box f64)", yet
construction takes the bare value and a match arm narrows to the
bare element -- no #~"Box" appears in user code:

#code language=zerolang: """
  v: (json.Value.intval 42)   # construction takes the bare i64
  match (v)
  case intval then print "\{v}"   # v narrows to the bare i64
  else print "not an int"
"""

#b:"Reading a tree." Element and member reads inside the tree are
borrows, which user code cannot return -- so the path accessors
(#~"at" and its typed wrappers) surface the terminal value as
scalars in a #~"Found": the kind arm plus the matching payload
slot. Strings are copied out; structure (array / object) is
reported by kind and length, and is navigated in place by
matching on the tree at the use site.


#--: Index

#p: #b:"Types"

#ul:
  [#*: #> to=value: #b:"Value" -- #~"union { nul: null  boolean: (Box bool)  intval: (Box i64)  floatval: (Box f64)  text: String  array: Arr  object: Obj }"]
  [#*: #> to=arr: #b:"Arr" -- #~"class { items: (List Value) }"]
  [#*: #> to=obj: #b:"Obj" -- #~"class { entries: (Map String V: Value)  keys: (List String) } as { set  has }"]
  [#*: #> to=jsonerror: #b:"JsonError" -- #~"class { at: u64  msg: String }"]
  [#*: #> to=parseresult: #b:"ParseResult" -- #~"union { err: JsonError  ok: Value }"]
  [#*: #> to=foundkind: #b:"foundkind" -- #~"variant { missing / nul / boolean / intval / floatval / text / array / object: null }"]
  [#*: #> to=found: #b:"Found" -- #~"class { kind: foundkind  b: bool  i: i64  f: f64  s: String  n: u64 }"]

#p: #b:"Parse and serialize"

#ul:
  [#*: #> to=parse: #b:"json.parse" -- #~"function {source: StringView} out ParseResult"]
  [#*: #> to=serialize: #b:"json.serialize" -- #~"function {v: Value.view} out String"]

#p: #b:"Path accessors"

#ul:
  [#*: #> to=at: #b:"json.at" -- #~"function {v: Value.view path: StringView} out Found"]
  [#*: #> to=describe-value: #b:"json.describeValue" -- #~"function {v: Value.view} out Found"]
  [#*: #> to=text-at: #b:"json.textAt" -- #~"function {v: Value.view path: StringView} out (Option String)"]
  [#*: #> to=int-at: #b:"json.intAt" -- #~"function {v: Value.view path: StringView} out (optionval i64)"]
  [#*: #> to=bool-at: #b:"json.boolAt" -- #~"function {v: Value.view path: StringView} out (optionval bool)"]
  [#*: #> to=has-at: #b:"json.hasAt" -- #~"function {v: Value.view path: StringView} out bool"]
  [#*: #> to=array-length: #b:"json.arrayLength" -- #~"function {v: Value.view} out u64"]
  [#*: #> to=found-missing: #b:"json.foundMissing" -- #~"function out Found"]


#--: value

#code language=zerolang: """
  Value: union {
      nul:      null
      boolean:  (Box bool)
      intval:   (Box i64)
      floatval: (Box f64)
      text:     String
      array:    Arr
      object:   Obj
  }
"""

A JSON value: a tagged sum of null / bool / int / float / string
/ array / object. The scalar arms follow the #~"(Box T)" arm
rule (a union holds reftypes; the Box argument collapses to its
element), #~"text" holds an owned #~"String", and the two
structured arms hold the #> to=arr: #~"Arr" and #> to=obj:
#~"Obj" carrier classes.

Integers and floats are deliberately separate arms: a JSON number
without #~"." or an exponent parses to #~"intval" and serializes
back digit-for-digit, so 64-bit ids survive a round trip that a
single #~"f64" arm would corrupt.

#b:"Construction" goes through the arm accessors; the collapsed
arms take the bare scalar:

#code language=zerolang: """
  a: json.Value.nul
  b: (json.Value.boolean true)
  n: (json.Value.intval 42)
  t: (json.Value.text "hi".string)
"""

#b:"Matching" narrows the subject to the arm's element type --
the bare scalar for the collapsed arms, the carrier class for
#~"array" / #~"object":

#code language=zerolang: """
  match (v)
  case intval then print "int \{v}"          # v is the bare i64
  case text   then print "text \{v}"         # v is the String payload
  case array  then print "\{v.items.length} elements"
  case nul    then print "null"
  else print "something else"
"""


#--: arr

#code language=zerolang: """
  Arr: class {
      items: (List Value)
  }
"""

A JSON array -- an ordered list of #~"Value"s. The #~"items"
field is public: append, index and iterate it directly with the
#> to=./stdlib_collections.html#the-list-family: #~"List" surface.

#code language=zerolang: """
  a: (json.Arr items: (List json.Value))
  a.items.append (json.Value.intval 1)
  a.items.append (json.Value.intval 2)
  v: (json.Value.array a)
"""


#--: obj

#code language=zerolang: """
  Obj: class {
      entries: (Map String V: Value)
      keys:    (List String)
  } as {
      set: function {:this key: String.take value: Value.take}
      has: function {:this key: StringView} out bool
  }
"""

A JSON object -- string-keyed #~"Value"s kept in insertion
order. #~"entries" gives O(1) lookup; #~"keys" remembers first
insertion order, which is the order #> to=serialize: #~"serialize"
emits.

#ul:
  [#*: #~"set key: value:" -- insert or replace #~"key". A first
    insertion records the key in #~"keys"; a replacement keeps
    the original position]
  [#*: #~"has key:" -- whether #~"key" is present, looked up by
    view]

#code language=zerolang: """
  o: (json.Obj entries: (Map String V: json.Value) keys: (List String))
  o.set key: "answer".string value: (json.Value.intval 42)
  o.set key: "label".string  value: (json.Value.text "hi".string)
  root: (json.Value.object o)
  print (json.serialize v: root)   # {"answer":42,"label":"hi"}
"""


#--: jsonerror

#code language=zerolang: """
  JsonError: class {
      at:  u64
      msg: String
  }
"""

A parse failure: #~"at" is the byte offset into the input where
the parser stopped; #~"msg" is a short description
(#~"unexpected character", #~"expected ',' or '}'",
#~"unterminated string", #~"bad escape", #~"unpaired surrogate",
#~"bad number", #~"nesting too deep", #~"trailing data", ...).
Only the first failure is recorded.


#--: parseresult

#code language=zerolang: """
  ParseResult: union {
      err: JsonError
      ok:  Value
  }
"""

The outcome of parsing JSON text: #~"ok" carries the root
#~"Value", #~"err" a #> to=jsonerror: #~"JsonError". A dedicated
union rather than a #~"Result" instantiation, matched the same
way (falsy arm first).


#--: foundkind

#code language=zerolang: """
  foundkind: variant {
      missing:  null
      nul:      null
      boolean:  null
      intval:   null
      floatval: null
      text:     null
      array:    null
      object:   null
  }
"""

Which value kind a path lookup landed on, or #~"missing" when
the path does not resolve. One arm per #~"Value" arm plus
#~"missing"; all payloads are #~"null", so a #~"foundkind" is a
plain inline discriminator.


#--: found

#code language=zerolang: """
  Found: class {
      kind: foundkind
      b:    bool
      i:    i64
      f:    f64
      s:    String
      n:    u64
  }
"""

A scalar path-lookup result: the #~"kind" plus the matching
payload slot. Only the slot named by #~"kind" is meaningful --
#~"b" for #~"boolean", #~"i" for #~"intval", #~"f" for
#~"floatval", #~"s" for #~"text" (an owned copy of the string),
and #~"n" for #~"array" / #~"object" (the element count / key
count). The other slots hold zero values.

#~"Found" exists because reads inside the tree are borrows that
cannot leave the call chain; copying the terminal scalar out
gives the caller an unencumbered value. Structure is reported by
kind and length only -- navigate an array or object in place by
matching on the tree.


#--: parse

#code language=zerolang: """
  parse: function {source: StringView} out ParseResult
"""

Whole-input parse of #~"source"; trailing non-whitespace after
the root value is an error. Any JSON value may be the root -- an
object, array, string, number, boolean, or #~"null".

#ul:
  [#*: #b:"Nesting" is capped at 128 levels of arrays / objects
    (#~"nesting too deep")]
  [#*: #b:"Strings" handle the RFC 8259 escapes #~"\\\"",
    #~"\\\\", #~"\\/", #~"\\b", #~"\\f", #~"\\n", #~"\\r",
    #~"\\t" and #~"\\uXXXX", including surrogate pairs; an
    unpaired surrogate, a bad escape, or a raw control byte in a
    string is an error]
  [#*: #b:"Numbers" without #~"." or an exponent become
    #~"intval" and must fit #~"i64" (overflow is a parse error);
    anything with #~"." / #~"e" / #~"E" becomes #~"floatval" via
    #~"f64" parsing]
  [#*: #b:"Duplicate keys" follow #~"Obj.set": the later value
    replaces the earlier one, at the key's original position]

#code language=zerolang: """
  pr: json.parse source: "[1, 2, 3]"
  match (pr)
  case ok then {
      n: json.arrayLength v: pr
      print "\{n} elements"
  }
  case err then print "parse error at byte \{pr.at}: \{pr.msg}"
"""


#--: serialize

#code language=zerolang: """
  serialize: function {v: Value.view} out String
"""

Serialize #~"v" to compact JSON text: no whitespace, object keys
in insertion order, strings escaped per RFC 8259 (#~"\"",
#~"\\", backspace, form feed, newline, carriage return and tab
as named escapes; other control bytes as #~"\\u00XX"; multi-byte
UTF-8 passed through verbatim). Returns a fresh owned
#~"String".

Because objects keep insertion order, #~"parse" -->
#~"serialize" round-trips a document key-for-key, and building
the same tree twice serializes to identical text.


#--: at

#code language=zerolang: """
  at: function {v: Value.view path: StringView} out Found
"""

Walk #~"path" from #~"v" and describe the terminal: a
#~"/"-separated sequence of segments, where a segment names an
object key and a numeric segment indexes an array
(#~"params/textDocument/uri", #~"tags/0"). Returns a
#> to=found: #~"Found" -- kind #~"missing" when any step fails
to resolve (absent key, index out of range, or a scalar where
structure was expected).

#code language=zerolang: """
  r: json.at v: root path: "result/items/0/label"
  k: r.kind
  match (k)
  case text    then print "label: \{r.s}"
  case missing then print "no label"
  else print "unexpected kind"
"""

The typed wrappers below cover the common cases; reach for
#~"at" itself when you need the kind, or a length without
matching on the tree.


#--: describe_value

#code language=zerolang: """
  describeValue: function {v: Value.view} out Found
"""

One #~"Value" as a #> to=found: #~"Found", with no path step:
the kind arm plus the matching payload slot (string copied out;
array / object reported as kind plus length in #~"n").


#--: text_at

#code language=zerolang: """
  textAt: function {v: Value.view path: StringView} out (Option String)
"""

The terminal string at #~"path", as an owned copy. #~"none" when
the path does not resolve or the terminal is not a string.

#code language=zerolang: """
  nm: json.textAt v: root path: "name"
  match (nm)
  case some then print "name: \{nm}"
  case none then print "name missing"
"""


#--: int_at

#code language=zerolang: """
  intAt: function {v: Value.view path: StringView} out (optionval i64)
"""

The terminal integer at #~"path". #~"none" when the path does
not resolve or the terminal is not an #~"intval" (a float is not
silently truncated).


#--: bool_at

#code language=zerolang: """
  boolAt: function {v: Value.view path: StringView} out (optionval bool)
"""

The terminal boolean at #~"path". #~"none" when the path does
not resolve or the terminal is not a #~"boolean".


#--: has_at

#code language=zerolang: """
  hasAt: function {v: Value.view path: StringView} out bool
"""

Whether #~"path" resolves to any value at all -- of any kind,
including #~"null".


#--: array_length

#code language=zerolang: """
  arrayLength: function {v: Value.view} out u64
"""

Element count of #~"v", or #~"0" when #~"v" is not an array.
Takes the value itself, not a path -- combine with a #~"match"
on an #~"array" arm, or use #> to=at: #~"at" and read #~"n".


#--: found_missing

#code language=zerolang: """
  foundMissing: function out Found
"""

A #~"Found" marking "no such path": kind #~"missing", every
payload slot at its zero value. This is what #~"at" returns for
an unresolved path; useful as a default when building your own
lookups over a tree.


#--: A worked example

Parse a small document, walk it with the path accessors, and
serialize it back:

#code language=zerolang: """
  src: "{\"name\": \"ada\", \"port\": 8080, \"tags\": [\"x\", \"y\"]}"
  pr: json.parse source: src
  match (pr)
  case ok then {
      nm: json.textAt v: pr path: "name"
      match (nm)
      case some then print "name: \{nm}"
      case none then print "name missing"

      pt: json.intAt v: pr path: "port"
      match (pt)
      case some then print "port: \{pt}"
      case none then print "port missing"

      t0: json.textAt v: pr path: "tags/0"
      match (t0)
      case some then print "first tag: \{t0}"
      case none then print "no tags"

      out: json.serialize v: pr
      print out       # {"name":"ada","port":8080,"tags":["x","y"]}
  }
  case err then print "parse error at byte \{pr.at}: \{pr.msg}"
"""

Inside the #~"case ok" arm the bound name #~"pr" narrows to the
root #~"Value"; inside #~"case some" each bound option narrows
to its payload. The string literal is a #~"StringView", which is
exactly what #~"parse" takes.
